From 9428b9c4c7371f4e8c7121b13f05b3d9e3a78914 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 03:34:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] repack: dual residency measured - both wins held; fix the
 hot-path cost that ate the first one

The design landed and the first implementation still lost. Recording both, because the second is
the more useful result.

MEASURED (RIG-A, llama-server, two g64 models, -ngl 0 --no-host -c 2048 -t 8, 519-token prefill,
n_predict 128, temp 0, three interleaved replicates x 5 rolls, roll 1 dropped, n=12 per cell, one
binary with only the env flip, free RAM 5.60-5.72 GB at every launch):

  prompt tok/s   off      mode 1              mode 2 (dual)
  mixed          898.11   310.65  (-65.4%)    958.96
  all-Q2_0       905.25   178.25  (-80.3%)    963.51
  decode tok/s
  mixed           40.30    49.53  (+22.9%)     49.16  (+22.0%)
  all-Q2_0        40.95    50.95  (+24.4%)     50.65  (+23.7%)

Every percentage above has non-overlapping ON/OFF ranges and a gap wider than the largest
within-arm spread. The prompt gain for mode 2 is deliberately NOT claimed - those ranges overlap,
so the honest statement is that dual residency does not lose prefill, not that it gains any.
Decode reaches mode 1's win to within the noise, while mode 1 pays -65% to -80% of prefill for it.

THE REGRESSION, AND WHY IT IS IN THE COMMIT MESSAGE. The first version passed the entire
correctness sweep - bit-identical to mode 1, placement identical to mode 0 - and measured SLOWER
THAN DOING NOTHING: decode 40.9 -> 24.7 on the all-Q2_0 model. Footprint was ruled out first, since
mode 1 holds the same bytes and is the fastest arm. The cost was per-call overhead:
get_tensor_traits and the weight-base helper run once per MUL_MAT per WORKER THREAD (~1.4k per
token at 8 threads, ggml/src/ggml-cpu/traits.cpp:12), and that path carried two getenv()s plus
strstr()s over the tensor name, an EXCLUSIVE unique_lock serialising all eight workers on every
matmul, a shared_lock plus an unordered_map hash, and an unconditional seq_cst atomic exchange
added for the accounting line.

Fixed by resolving everything once, at shadow-build time, into tensor->extra - ggml's per-tensor
backend-private slot, which the CPU_REPACK buffer already uses for exactly this. The steady-state
path is now a field-read predicate and one pointer load. The eligibility check is split into
_candidate (field reads only, safe per call) and _eligible (the expensive part, once per tensor).
The accounting line moved off the hot path into the builder; the cost is that its last INFO line
understates the settled total by up to 64 MiB, which is stated in the code rather than hidden.

Every reader of ->extra was checked before reusing it: CPU_REPACK's set_tensor and the traits fast
path both gate on the repack buffer type, which mode-2 tensors never match; AMX, KleidiAI and
SpaceMiT set it only for their own non-host buffer types, which fail the candidate predicate; and
the two sites that COPY extra to a derived tensor would hand over a shadow whose src_data no longer
matches, which the assert catches as an abort rather than as wrong output.

Docs land in the same unit of work: FINDINGS F-09 exit 1 goes from "this is the direction" to built
and measured, CHANGELOG gains the entry, and the OPTIONS-REGISTRY row carries the full protocol,
the cost, the declared UNEXERCISED classes (Q1_0, in-process reload, running without --no-host,
discrete GPUs, Arm) and the regression above.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110E, F-09 exit 1
Measured-effect: decode +22.0% (mixed) and +23.7% (all-Q2_0) against mode 0, ranges DISJOINT, while
  prefill is held at baseline instead of collapsing -65%/-80% as mode 1 does. Shadow cost 4.68 /
  84.16 / 95.98 MiB on the 4- / 72- / 168-tensor models, byte-for-byte equal to what mode 1 places
  in CPU_REPACK. Precision unchanged: with --no-op-offload, which isolates the shadow from where
  prefill ran, mode 2 is bit-identical to mode 1 on all six model x prompt cells (max |delta
  logprob| 0.000000000, deterministic 3/3). Re-verified on the shipped binary after the hot-path
  fix. Evidence: research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/dual-residency/
